fix(editor): agent writes could silently revert user edits — external-change detection

Problem: user edits a file in the KAI editor while the AI agent changes the
same file on disk. Saving from the editor blindly overwrote the file,
reverting all agent changes with no warning. Open editors also never
reloaded after auto-approved agent writes (only ai-diff approvals did,
via a per-approval effect in App.tsx).

Fix — three layers:

Rust (fs/file.rs):
- fs_write_file + fs_write_file_bytes emit `fs-changed` with the written
  path (forward-slash normalized) after every successful atomic write.
- Process-global AppHandle (OnceLock) set in Builder::setup lets sync
  fs commands emit without signature changes.

useDocument (editor/lib/useDocument.ts):
- Listens for `fs-changed`; matching path + clean buffer -> auto-reload;
  matching path + dirty buffer -> conflict state, buffer untouched.
- Own writes are ignored (selfWriteRef spans the async event dispatch).
- save() is now guarded: on conflict it refuses and returns
  { ok: false, conflict: true } instead of clobbering disk. Even without
  a prior event, it re-reads disk content and compares against the
  last-known-saved snapshot - divergence = conflict, no blind overwrite.
- New actions: takeDiskVersion (reload, discarding user edits) and
  overwriteDisk (write user buffer, discarding external change).
- SaveOutcome: { ok: true } | { ok:false, conflict:true } |
  { ok:false, conflict:false, error }.

EditorPane:
- Amber conflict banner over the editor: "File changed on disk" with
  Reload (discard my edits) and Overwrite with my edits buttons; error
  surface for failed overwrite.
- Mod-s, vim :w, and the imperative handle save() all route through
  saveAndReport - onSaved fires only on success, conflict keeps the
  banner up.

App.tsx:
- Removed the ai-diff per-approval reload effect - superseded by the
  fs-changed listener, which also covers auto-approved writes the old
  effect missed.

Verified: cargo check clean, tsc clean, 254 vitest tests pass.
